    <h1>Ravens in Old Norse Kennings</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Skaldic circumlocutions — the raven as poetic building block</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse skaldic poetry uses <strong>kennings</strong> — two-part compound circumlocutions that replace direct words. Ravens are both subjects of kennings ("Huginn's kin," "wound-bird") and bases for kennings about warriors, swords, and battles ("feeder of ravens" = warrior; "raven's feast" = battle). The raven is one of the most frequent kenning elements in the surviving skaldic corpus.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What Is a Kenning?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> A kenning (<em>kenning</em>, ON <em>kenning</em> from <em>kenna</em> "to know; to refer to") is a two-element compound phrase that substitutes for a single noun in Old Norse (and Old English) poetry:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>brimfugll</em> ("surf-bird") = seagull</li>
      <li><em>hrönn-sær</em> ("wave-pig") = ship</li>
      <li><em>bein-sker</em> ("bone-cutter") = sword</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Kennings can be extended into "hyperkennings" of three or more elements. They were the central ornament of skaldic verse — a poet's skill was measured in part by the complexity and aptness of their kennings. Snorri Sturluson's <em>Skáldskaparmál</em> (Prose Edda, c. 1220) catalogues kenning types systematically.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Kennings for the Raven Itself</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The raven can be referred to by kennings that describe its associations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>sárfugll</em> — "wound-bird" (sár = wound; fugll = bird)</li>
      <li><em>valgefn</em> — "corpse-giver" or "battle-gift bird" (val = the slain; gefa = to give)</li>
      <li><em>Munins niðr</em> — "kinsman of Muninn" (kinsman of Odin's raven)</li>
      <li><em>Hugins vinr</em> — "friend of Huginn"</li>
      <li><em>valþjófr</em> — "corpse-thief" or "battle-thief"</li>
      <li>Various compounds involving <em>val-</em> (the battle-slain) + bird element</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These kennings emphasise the raven's role as a carrion feeder at the battlefield — the defining cultural association of the raven in Old Norse warrior culture.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Kennings for Warrior Using "Raven"</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> A warrior can be kenned as "feeder of [ravens/wolves/eagles]" — the beast-of-battle formula applied to kenning structure:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>hrafnblóðgari</em> — "raven-bloodier" (one who provides ravens with blood to drink)</li>
      <li>"Feeder of Huginn" — a warrior who feeds Odin's raven with corpses</li>
      <li>"Raven-gladder" — a warrior whose fighting gladdens the ravens</li>
      <li>"Raven-satisfier" or "raven-pleaser" — along the same line</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This type of kenning frames a warrior's prowess in terms of the ecological consequence of their combat skill — their victories produce raven-fodder. It is a compliment cast in the language of carrion ecology.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Kennings for Battle Using "Raven"</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Battle can be kenned as the raven's feast or opportunity:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"Raven's feast" / "raven's banquet" — battle as a meal for ravens</li>
      <li>"Raven-joy" — battle as something that pleases ravens</li>
      <li>"Meeting of ravens" — a kenning that places the bird (and its associations) at the heart of the conflict</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These kennings draw on the same ecological reality — ravens appearing at large-scale violence — elevated into the ornamental language of court poetry.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Kennings for Odin Using "Raven"</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Odin himself is kenned using his raven associations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Hrafnguðr</em> — "Raven-god" (a byname, also interpretable as a kenning base)</li>
      <li>"Master of Huginn and Muninn"</li>
      <li>"He who owns the two ravens"</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The raven-based kennings for Odin reinforce his identity as the god whose intelligence comes through his birds. See the <a href="/questions/what-is-the-norse-word-for-raven/">Norse word for raven</a> for Odin's full byname list.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

